# SYNOPSIS

```usage
date [-u] [-d time] [+format | mmddHHMM[[CC]yy]]
```

# DESCRIPTION

`date` prints the date and time in the format `%a %b %e %H:%M:%S %Z %Y`: the day, month, day of the month, time, zone and year.
An argument beginning `+` gives the format instead: the rest of it is `strftime`'s, in which `%Y`, `%m`, `%d`, `%H`, `%M`, `%S`, `%a`, `%b`, `%Z`, `%s` and the like are replaced and other characters printed as they are.

An argument of digits sets the clock instead: <mm> the month, <dd> the day, <HH> the hour and <MM> the minute, then optionally <yy> the year (69 to 99 in the 1900s, 00 to 68 in the 2000s) or <CC><yy> the century and year.

: `-u`
  Print the time in UTC.
: `-d` <time>
  Print <time>, a number of seconds since 1970-01-01 00:00 UTC, instead of now.

The time is the wall clock, the kernel's clock plus the offset a clock driver such as drv-rtc-cmos(3) sets (see clock(2)); before one has, or with none, it is in January 1970.
With no `TZ` in the environment the time is printed in UTC, as there is no time zone database.

`date` is sbase's, a hard link to `/boot/bin/posix/sbase-box`; there is no native one.
In the POSIX namespace (a manifest's `ns=posix`, made by `/lib/ns/posix`, see namespace(6)) `/boot/bin/posix` is bound before `/boot/bin` on `/bin`, so a POSIX program finds it as `/bin/date`.

# SEE ALSO

cal(1), clock(2), drv-rtc-cmos(3); sbase's page is `third_party/sbase/date.1` in the repository

# DIAGNOSTICS

An unknown flag, a missing <time>, or more than one argument prints the usage message and exits 1.
A malformed date to set is reported, as `date: invalid date format: 13`, and a <time> that is not a number as `date: strtonum x: invalid`; each exits 1.

# BUGS

Setting the date always fails on VectraOS, as `date: clock_settime: Function not implemented`: the C library does not implement `clock_settime`, though the kernel has clock_set(2).

`-d` takes seconds, not the date string GNU's `date -d` does, and is not in POSIX.
A formatted date longer than 1023 bytes is not printed: `strftime` fails, and what is left in the buffer is printed instead.
`TZ` and `-u` are untested on VectraOS.
